        "y2": 1520.0
      },
      "layout_tag": "paragraph",
      "confidence": 0.7899107933044434,
      "reading_order": 7,
      "text": "2. The figure represents Thomson’s atomic model. From Thomson’s experiment, it can be con- cluded that electrons are the universal constitu- ent of matter."
    },
    {
      "block_id": "20260317_18da70d8-38fc-4407-831c-8d2ad06d65ae_7_block_007",
      "coordinates": {
        "x1": 292.0,
        "y1": 1540.0,
        "x2": 883.0,
        "y2": 1594.0
      },
      "layout_tag": "paragraph",
      "confidence": 0.5237067341804504,
      "reading_order": 8,
      "text": "Hence, the correct option is (d)."
    },
    {
      "block_id": "20260317_18da70d8-38fc-4407-831c-8d2ad06d65ae_7_block_008",
      "coordinates": {
        "x1": 236.0,
        "y1": 1619.0,
        "x2": 1168.0,
        "y2": 1998.0
      },
      "layout_tag": "paragraph",
      "confidence": 0.9356362819671631,
      "reading_order": 9,
      "text": "3. The energy of orbits increases with distance\nfrom the nucleus in the order K < L < M < N.\nEnergy is released when an electron jumps\nfrom a higher energy orbit to a lower energy\norbit. Among the given transitions, such transi-\ntions from higher orbit to lower orbit are $\\mathrm{L} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{K}$,\n$\\mathrm{M} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{L}$, and $\\mathrm{N} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{K}$."
    },
    {
      "block_id": "20260317_18da70d8-38fc-4407-831c-8d2ad06d65ae_7_block_009",
      "coordinates": {
        "x1": 293.0,
        "y1": 2015.0,
        "x2": 877.0,
        "y2": 2069.0
      },
      "layout_tag": "paragraph",
      "confidence": 0.7721574902534485,
      "reading_order": 10,
      "text": "Hence, the correct option is (c)."
    },
    {
      "block_id": "20260317_18da70d8-38fc-4407-831c-8d2ad06d65ae_7_block_010",
      "coordinates": {
        "x1": 234.0,
        "y1": 2094.0,
        "x2": 1169.0,
        "y2": 2416.0
      },
      "layout_tag": "paragraph",
      "confidence": 0.9391432404518127,
      "reading_order": 11,
      "text": "4. The orbits in which electrons revolve around\nthe nucleus are called stationary orbits since\nthe energy of electrons remains constant in\nthose orbits. The electrons are in continuous\nmotion around the nucleus in those orbits and\nnever remain stationary."
    },
    {
      "block_id": "20260317_18da70d8-38fc-4407-831c-8d2ad06d65ae_7_block_011",
      "coordinates": {
        "x1": 290.0,
        "y1": 2436.0,
        "x2": 877.0,
        "y2": 2488.0
      },
      "layout_tag": "paragraph",
      "confidence": 0.7000882625579834,
      "reading_order": 12,
      "text": "Hence, the correct option is (c)."
    },
    {
      "block_id": "20260317_18da70d8-38fc-4407-831c-8d2ad06d65ae_7_block_012",
      "coordinates": {
        "x1": 233.0,
